Title:
Accelerating EDR Evasion with LLM-Driven Analysis

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
This post describes a practical workflow for using LLMs plus reversing tooling to rapidly analyze and evade endpoint detections, using Palo Alto Cortex XDR as the primary example. It shows how a simple “looped” harness (Codex-CLI in Docker + Binary Ninja via MCP + persistent markdown state) can iteratively reverse local EDR components and extract actionable detection logic. The author demonstrates offline recovery of shipped YARA rules (AES-wrapped and compressed), reconstruction of behavioral detection metadata (DSE/BIOC rules), and extraction/execution of local ML models (tree-ensemble scorers with feature extractors). It also details decrypting CLP/CLIPS-based rule blobs to reveal allow/deny logic that can be used to craft bypasses (e.g., command/path allowlists for `reg save` SAM export). The piece is primarily useful for red teams, malware/tool developers, and security researchers validating how much “on-host” detection logic can be enumerated and tested offline. It’s notable because it operationalizes LLM-assisted reversing into a repeatable pipeline that turns vendor endpoint content into concrete evasion hypotheses and test harnesses quickly.

Technical Focus:
- LLM-driven reverse engineering harnesses (loop/stateful workflows)
- User-mode EDR hooking/injection analysis (ntdll/APC/loader hooks)
- Offline extraction/decryption of local YARA rulesets (AES + compression)
- Behavioral rule reconstruction (DSE/BIOC metadata, child-process patterns)
- Local ML model extraction and scoring harnesses (serialized decision trees, feature engineering)
- CLIPS/CLP rule blob decryption and policy/allowlist discovery

Use Cases:
- Rapidly enumerate and diff an EDR’s shipped local signatures/rules across versions
- Build offline test harnesses to reproduce detections and validate bypass ideas safely
- Identify high-leverage bypass points (e.g., ntdll remap/direct syscalls/manual map) from hook maps
- Extract and analyze local ML feature pipelines to guide “feature suppression” style evasion
- Discover allowlist/exception logic embedded in rule languages (e.g., CLIPS) to craft low-noise tradecraft

Keywords:
EDR evasion, LLM-assisted reversing, Cortex XDR, Palo Alto, Binary Ninja, Codex-CLI, MCP, DLL injection, inline hooks, LdrInitializeThunk, KiUserApcDispatcher, NtContinue, direct syscalls, ntdll remapping, YARA, AES-128-ECB, AES-256-CBC, gzip inflate, behavioral detections, BIOC, DSE rules, child process regex, ML plugin, decision tree ensemble, feature extraction, CLIPS, CLP decryption, reg save HKLM\\SAM, Mythic agent, BloodHound, LDAP monitoring